1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:11,610
Here is a cube whose eight corners each carry a name made of three bits.

2
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:18,970
So the corner at the front left is zero zero zero, and the corner above it is zero one zero.

3
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:25,740
Now we join two corners by an edge exactly when their names differ in one single bit.

4
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:32,662
So zero zero zero and zero zero one are neighbours, since only the last bit changes.

5
00:00:32,662 --> 00:00:36,680
In all, the cube has twelve such edges.

6
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:43,876
But zero zero zero and zero one one differ in two bits, so no edge joins them.

7
00:00:43,876 --> 00:00:48,537
That means a corner has one neighbour for every bit that can be flipped,

8
00:00:48,537 --> 00:00:51,580
which is three neighbours for each corner here.

9
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:58,066
With n bits the same rule gives two to the n corners, and each one has n neighbours.

10
00:00:58,066 --> 00:01:01,976
The Connection Machine of the nineteen eighties used twenty bits,

11
00:01:01,976 --> 00:01:06,610
so it joined about a million processors that each had only twenty neighbours.

12
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:12,630
Wiring every pair directly would have needed about half a trillion wires.

13
00:01:13,483 --> 00:01:16,385
Now look at the same cube a second way.

14
00:01:16,385 --> 00:01:23,851
The four corners whose names start with zero form a square at the front, and we call it the zero subcube.

15
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:31,040
The four corners whose names start with one form a second square at the back, which is the one subcube.

16
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:38,402
Each square is a two dimensional hypercube, and the first bit is simply copied onto every name.

17
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:44,596
The four edges left over join each corner of the front square to the corner behind it,

18
00:01:44,596 --> 00:01:46,827
which has the same last two bits.

19
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:55,093
So zero one zero is joined to one one zero, and the first bit is the only one that changes.

20
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:57,612
That is the second definition,

21
00:01:57,612 --> 00:02:03,596
which takes two copies of the smaller hypercube and joins every corner to its twin.

22
00:02:03,596 --> 00:02:11,036
So the cube has twelve edges, which is twice the four edges of a square plus four joining edges.

23
00:02:11,916 --> 00:02:16,021
Lemma ten point one counts the edges of the hypercube,

24
00:02:16,021 --> 00:02:20,354
and it says the answer is n times two to the n minus one.

25
00:02:20,354 --> 00:02:28,216
We will prove it twice, once from each definition, and the cube with its twelve edges is our test case.

26
00:02:28,583 --> 00:02:35,165
The first proof uses the direct definition, in which every corner has exactly n neighbours,

27
00:02:35,165 --> 00:02:37,841
one for each bit that can be flipped.

28
00:02:37,841 --> 00:02:42,661
So in our cube each of the eight corners has degree three.

29
00:02:43,016 --> 00:02:49,112
Adding up the degrees counts every edge twice, once from each of its two ends.

30
00:02:49,112 --> 00:02:54,748
So eight times three is twenty four ends, and half of that is twelve edges.

31
00:02:54,748 --> 00:03:03,720
In general that is n times two to the n, divided by two, which is n times two to the n minus one.

32
00:03:04,083 --> 00:03:08,399
The second proof uses the recursive definition instead.

33
00:03:08,399 --> 00:03:14,175
A hypercube is two smaller hypercubes plus the edges joining twins,

34
00:03:14,175 --> 00:03:21,417
so the edge count E of n equals twice E of n minus one, plus two to the n minus one.

35
00:03:21,783 --> 00:03:27,663
Start from one edge for the one dimensional cube, and the rule gives four for the square.

36
00:03:27,663 --> 00:03:35,391
Then it gives twelve for the cube, and one more step gives thirty two, which matches four times two cubed.

37
00:03:35,750 --> 00:03:41,140
To prove it for every n, assume the formula holds for one fewer bit,

38
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:46,056
so doubling it gives n minus one times two to the n minus one.

39
00:03:46,056 --> 00:03:54,802
Then adding the joining edges, two to the n minus one more, makes it n times two to the n minus one.

40
00:03:55,683 --> 00:04:00,709
Now ask how hard it is to cut a group of corners away from the rest of the cube.

41
00:04:00,709 --> 00:04:07,367
Take just the corner zero zero zero, and the three edges leaving it must all be cut.

42
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:13,626
Take instead all four corners of the front square, which is the zero subcube.

43
00:04:13,626 --> 00:04:18,958
Now exactly four edges leave it, and those are the four joining edges,

44
00:04:18,958 --> 00:04:21,930
so four edges are cut for four corners.

45
00:04:22,283 --> 00:04:26,601
Theorem ten point five says that this always happens.

46
00:04:26,601 --> 00:04:34,761
Whenever S holds at most half of all the corners, at least as many edges leave S as S has corners.

47
00:04:35,616 --> 00:04:39,158
We prove it by induction on the number of bits.

48
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:47,256
With one bit there are two corners and one edge, so a set of one corner is cut off by exactly that one edge.

49
00:04:47,616 --> 00:04:53,994
For the step, split S into S zero in the front subcube and S one in the back one,

50
00:04:53,994 --> 00:04:56,514
where S zero is the larger part.

51
00:04:56,514 --> 00:05:03,378
Then either both parts are at most half of their subcube, or S zero is more than half.

52
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:09,104
In the first case the hypothesis applies inside each subcube separately.

53
00:05:09,104 --> 00:05:16,423
So at least two edges leave S zero inside the front, and at least two leave S one inside the back,

54
00:05:16,423 --> 00:05:20,308
which gives at least four, as many as S has corners.

55
00:05:20,683 --> 00:05:25,408
In the second case S zero is more than half of the front subcube,

56
00:05:25,408 --> 00:05:28,897
so the hypothesis cannot be used on it directly.

57
00:05:28,897 --> 00:05:33,150
In our cube S zero has three corners and S one has one,

58
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:37,791
which is small enough to give at least one edge in the back.

59
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:45,230
The trick is to look at the front corners outside S zero, which here is the single corner zero one one.

60
00:05:45,230 --> 00:05:52,350
That set is small, so the hypothesis gives at least one edge between it and S zero.

61
00:05:52,716 --> 00:05:59,816
Finally every corner of S zero has a twin behind it, and at most one of those twins is in S one.

62
00:05:59,816 --> 00:06:07,655
So at least three minus one, which is two, joining edges cross, and one plus one plus two is four,

63
00:06:07,655 --> 00:06:09,814
the number of corners of S.

64
00:06:09,814 --> 00:06:16,816
In general the three parts add up to two to the k, and that is at least the size of S.

